## How Gson Resolves TypeAdapters and Prevents Infinite Recursion

### File & Symbol

**`gson/src/main/java/com/google/gson/Gson.java`**
- **`Gson.getAdapter(TypeToken<T>)`** — the core resolution method (lines 319–378)
- **`Gson.FutureTypeAdapter<T>`** — the placeholder that breaks cyclic resolution (lines 1216–1253)
- **`Gson.threadLocalAdapterResults`** — the per-thread in-progress map (line 151)
- **`Gson.typeTokenCache`** — the global completed-adapter cache (line 154)

---

### Step-by-step resolution

#### 1. Fast path — global cache
```java
TypeAdapter<?> cached = typeTokenCache.get(type);
if (cached != null) return cached;          // already resolved, done
```
`typeTokenCache` is a `ConcurrentHashMap` shared across all threads. If the adapter was resolved before, it is returned immediately.

#### 2. Thread-local in-progress map
```java
Map<TypeToken<?>, TypeAdapter<?>> threadCalls = threadLocalAdapterResults.get();
boolean isInitialAdapterRequest = false;
if (threadCalls == null) {
    threadCalls = new HashMap<>();
    threadLocalAdapterResults.set(threadCalls);
    isInitialAdapterRequest = true;
} else {
    TypeAdapter<T> ongoingCall = (TypeAdapter<T>) threadCalls.get(type);
    if (ongoingCall != null) return ongoingCall;   // ← cycle detected, return placeholder
}
```
`threadLocalAdapterResults` is a `ThreadLocal<Map<TypeToken<?>, TypeAdapter<?>>>`. On the **first** call on this thread it is created fresh (`isInitialAdapterRequest = true`). On **re-entrant** calls (same thread, same type) the map already contains an entry for that type, so the placeholder is returned immediately — this is what breaks the cycle.

#### 3. FutureTypeAdapter placeholder — the cycle-breaker
```java
FutureTypeAdapter<T> call = new FutureTypeAdapter<>();
threadCalls.put(type, call);                // register placeholder BEFORE iterating factories
```
Before asking any factory to create an adapter, Gson inserts a `
